          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus primarily identifies justification as God's forensic declaration or acquittal of a sinner through Christ, while also recognizing broader senses such as judicial vindication and demonstrating someone or something to be right. It notes disputes over imputation, inward transformation, covenant membership, and works.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book defines justification more generally as judging, declaring, or demonstrating a person, statement, deed, or cause to be right against a charge, in opposition to condemnation. In the sinner's case, it locates justification in forgiveness through Christ and distinguishes this from declaring wrongdoing itself to be right.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A forensic judgment by God that acquits or declares the sinner righteous and in right standing through Christ.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A forensic or vindicatory act that judges, declares, or demonstrates the accused person or matter to be right against a charge; for sinners, God does this through forgiveness in Christ.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_BROADENS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book extends the term beyond God's soteriological verdict upon sinners to the vindication of persons, words, deeds, causes, wisdom, and even God. It also includes demonstrative justification by works. Conversely, it treats forgiveness—not the commonly taught formula of imputed righteousness—as the defining explanation of how God justifies sinners, and it does not incorporate transformative justification into the lexical core.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction in the shared forensic case: acquittal or favorable judgment through Christ is compatible with the consensus core. The book's emphasis on forgiveness may qualify or displace imputation as the definition, but its warning against declaring wrongdoing right does not contradict mainstream forensic teaching, which likewise does not claim that wrongdoing becomes right.\",\"rationale\":\"The central category remains forensic vindication, so the book does not replace the consensus referent. However, it materially broadens the objects and contexts covered and reclassifies passages such as Matthew 11, Luke 7, and James 2 as ordinary vindicatory or demonstrative uses within one general definition. Its soteriological formulation is also more specifically tied to forgiveness than the consensus's usual imputation formula. These are substantive boundary and precision changes, making REFINED more appropriate than MATCH or DIVERGENT.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The blind responses already recognize judicial, vindicatory, and demonstrative senses, including nearly all of the book's cited examples; therefore one could argue that the book merely foregrounds an acknowledged lexical range and should be classified MATCH. REFINED is preferable because the consensus still makes God's saving declaration of the sinner its primary core, whereas the book makes that only one instance of a broader rule and gives forgiveness rather than imputation definitional priority.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
